WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.686
I think he both confirmed what I thought he would be like and also added something

00:00:04.686 --> 00:00:06.000
really quite different.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.420
I knew he would be, to some extent, slightly intimidating because he was such a

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.000
powerful intellect and he was such a powerful physical presence.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
And he didn't suffer falls.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
But also he came across as being extremely sympathetic and interested.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.836
And interested in me as then a young writer. I was spanning between the worlds of

00:00:25.836 --> 00:00:29.000
academic world, architectural history and journalism.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.632
Cedric was interested in what I was doing, what I was interested in, and what I knew

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:31.820
about.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.240
And that generosity, that inquisitiveness, I think, came across very strongly,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.540
more strongly than the fact that he was actually quite a formidable figure.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.100
So I liked him immediately.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.480
I thought I probably would, but I was also slightly nervous about meeting him.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.740
He certainly had a very strong physical presence.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.560
His appearance was very cultivated.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.680
He always wore a stiff white collar.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.680
He generally wore a black string knitted tie and often a black shirt,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.680
sometimes a light-coloured jacket.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.680
He was a big man, you know, he was my sort of build.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.680
He had a very powerful voice that he could make carry.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.680
He was theatrical, he could be quite a good mimic.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.680
He was a very good raconteur.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.680
You knew when he walked in a room.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.680
And he had that sort of charisma, that sort of presence,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.680
which was that of a performer and that of someone who had something to say.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:29.302
There was, and there still are, many things that Cedric said that still resonate in

00:01:29.302 --> 00:01:29.940
my head.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.600
There are phrases which sound simple but are actually incredibly complicated.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.260
And one of them is calculated uncertainty constructed.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.400
And if you think of those three words, they are, each of them, very precise.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.600
And you put them together and you get an extraordinary combination of meanings.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.180
But actually for Cedric, that was very, very precise.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:57.120
that it was you could build something architecture was not a futile act you

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.380
could calculate something so you could design you could predict you could think

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.880
about the future and obviously you had to deal with uncertainty you don't know

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.160
what's going to happen in the future you certainly don't know everything that's

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.120
going to happen in the future so whatever you do your calculations have

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.640
to take into account uncertainty so there are phrases like that which

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.380
sometimes to me are very useful even if they might be applied in very different

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.994
Delight, I think, was a very important term for Cedric. He used it in a number of

00:02:23.994 --> 00:02:28.380
different ways, and I think there are two in particular that I would mention.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.068
One is the translation of Vitruvius' text on architecture into English by a man

00:02:34.068 --> 00:02:37.380
called Henry Wotton in the early 17th century.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:45.117
It rendered commoditas, firmitas and venustas, the Latin words that Vitruvius used,

00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:48.380
as commodity, firmness and delight.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.797
Now, commodity and firmness come directly from commoditas and firmitas, that's quite

00:02:52.797 --> 00:02:53.060
easy.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.080
But coming from venustas to delight is more difficult,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.780
because venustas, of course, refers to Venus, the goddess of love, etc.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.025
Not something that Cedric would have been insensible to, he would have known the

00:03:03.025 --> 00:03:03.680
Latin as well.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.380
But delight, I think, for Cedric was important because of its reference to Wotton,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.560
one of the earliest, perhaps not quite the earliest,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.100
but one of the earliest texts in English on architecture, albeit a translation.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.614
so he was referring to something about the history of architecture, the history of

00:03:20.614 --> 00:03:21.660
architectural ideas

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.780
and the idea that architecture had a series of possibilities to offer

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.960
and part of those possibilities was delight and I think that was very important.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.800
But I think there's a second reason he used the word delight

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.206
and that's because it was a challenge to a particular form of English socialistic

00:03:38.206 --> 00:03:38.740
thinking

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.841
and Cedric of course was always on the left politically, he was an active supporter

00:03:42.841 --> 00:03:43.780
of the Labour Party

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.520
although he had friends from across the political spectrum.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.820
Now I think that for Cedric

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.600
delight was a challenge to the form of English socialism

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.060
encapsulated by people like J.B. Priestley, William Morris

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.820
Hugh Dalton, Stafford Cripps

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.880
who were both cabinet ministers in the Labour government

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.320
after the Second World War

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.740
which was very puritanical

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.940
and Cedric was certainly not a puritan

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.420
and so to say that something could be delightful

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.280
or it could engender the light,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.120
was a challenge to what would otherwise have become,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:22.060
was, I think, a very powerful form of socialist thinking.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.160
Cedric wasn't, he didn't accept convention.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.500
He would always challenge something if he thought it was wrong,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.280
even if his political beliefs were closer to Hugh Dalton than Margaret Thatcher.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.200
I think there are a number of ways or explanations

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.800
for Cedric being really influential.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:44.504
One is that I think the projects, both built and unbuilt, is a formidable body of

00:04:44.504 --> 00:04:44.880
work.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.420
And it is, of course, available.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.400
It's been available through publications more or less since it started.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.520
Both publications about architecture.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.600
He was obviously close to Architectural Design Magazine.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.360
There are various books and monographs published about him.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.780
So I think that's one reason.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.932
There is a formidable body of work that covers quite a wide range of what architects

00:05:03.932 --> 00:05:04.920
have to think about.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.478
Another reason, I think, is that he had a very powerful network of friends and

00:05:10.478 --> 00:05:11.140
associates.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.094
Now, David Alford, who was one of his closest friends, if not his closest architect

00:05:16.094 --> 00:05:16.460
friend,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.620
built an enormous amount.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.860
And in some ways, I think Cedric was the counterpart to David.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.640
David wanted to build.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:31.069
That was his raison d'etre, and he built some formidably good buildings of their

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:31.340
day,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.220
and quite a lot of them.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.880
Cedric I think was much less interested in building he was much more interested

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.880
in the possibilities of ideas that architectural designs could engender I

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.220
think another of the people who said Rick worked with closely and also had a

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.880
formidable built body of work many by other architects was the engineer Frank

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:55.800
newbie so Cedric was right at the heart of an architectural world that was

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.180
making a lot of buildings he was right at the heart of an architectural

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.860
publishing world and of course he was also associated with education. Students would

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.756
troop to go and see him even if he wasn't formally teaching them and quite often he

00:06:08.756 --> 00:06:09.460
would. You know

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.051
he made himself available. You had to be polite, you had to ask, you had to go

00:06:13.051 --> 00:06:13.880
through the formal

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:19.208
stages but it was very unusual for him to refuse to see someone point blank and I

00:06:19.208 --> 00:06:20.260
think there were

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:24.336
also the active teaching roles he had not least here at the Architectural

00:06:24.336 --> 00:06:25.620
Association. So I think

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.306
his ideas got out there in a number of different ways. I think it's a very difficult

00:06:30.306 --> 00:06:30.920
question to

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.488
answer whether Cedric really wanted to build because I think if he had he would have

00:06:34.488 --> 00:06:34.700
built

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.680
more. I think that he was interested in those buildings he wanted to build which is

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:40.280
not a very

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:44.850
large number and I think he was very interested in how you made the Avery for

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:45.800
example. I think

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.191
he was very interested in some of the smaller projects that did get built. I think

00:06:50.191 --> 00:06:50.780
he was very

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.911
interested in the InterAction Centre, particularly when the Fun Palace didn't go

00:06:54.911 --> 00:06:55.840
ahead. But I think

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.805
he would love to have built the Fun Palace, as would Joan Littlewood. The

00:06:59.805 --> 00:07:01.000
InterAction Centre was

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.040
one of the built projects that Cedric made in the 1970s. It was in a part of Kentish

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:06.340
Town,

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.146
then quite a poor part called, it was in a part of North London, then quite a poor

00:07:11.146 --> 00:07:11.780
part called

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:17.601
Kentish Town. And the idea was that you could create a series of containers or

00:07:17.601 --> 00:07:18.720
frames in which

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.811
all sorts of different things could happen. Some of them could be directly social,

00:07:22.811 --> 00:07:23.360
maybe a bit

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.331
cultural, maybe a bit educational, guided and unguided. I think Cedric was very

00:07:28.331 --> 00:07:28.960
interested

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:34.255
in setting up possibilities and allowing people to take their own path, but to give

00:07:34.255 --> 00:07:35.020
them various

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.963
opportunities or options for finding something out, educating themselves, having a

00:07:40.963 --> 00:07:41.760
bit of fun,

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:46.562
meeting other people. And that was what the InterAction Centre was about, I think,

00:07:46.562 --> 00:07:47.160
in essence,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.440
was to provide a frame in which people could do all sorts of different things,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.880
sometimes self-guided, sometimes on their own, sometimes communal.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.900
I think the InterAction Centre related to the Fun Palace in a number of ways.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.300
First of all, it clearly wasn't as ambitious.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:06.389
The Fun Palace was an extraordinary conception that came from Cedric and from Joan

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:06.980
Liverwood,

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:08.960
who was obviously a very important influence in it.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.653
The InterAction Centre provided some of the possibilities that the Fun Palace would

00:08:15.653 --> 00:08:16.620
have provided.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.520
I don't think the InterAction Centre would have been what it was

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.560
without the work that went into the Fun Palace beforehand.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.840
I don't think we see enough of Cedric in the built environment today.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:32.700
I think that a lot of what he said for much of his career is really important,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.560
which is that architects should think about the time of their buildings,

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.120
the life cycle of their buildings,

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.760
and they shouldn't try and make a monument that is made now for all eternity.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.760
One of Cedric's most important themes, I think, was about time.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.431
Time was very important for Cedric. The concept of time was very important for

00:08:51.431 --> 00:08:51.760
Cedric.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:57.760
And I think that one of the things he was critical of other architects for

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.760
was imposing their spatial imaginations, as I think he termed it,

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.760
and not much else on their clients,

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.367
with very little to explain why their spatial imaginations were better than anyone

00:09:08.367 --> 00:09:08.760
else's.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.760
Now, for Cedric, I think he could imagine in space, very definitely,

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:17.072
something like the Thinkbelt is an extraordinary conception of the way you use a

00:09:17.072 --> 00:09:17.880
piece of space,

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:19.440
quite a large piece of space in that case.

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.778
But he was also, and this is I think the essence, part of the essence of what Cedric

00:09:23.778 --> 00:09:24.240
was about,

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.060
he could also imagine in time.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.620
He could imagine how something might evolve over time

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.540
and he could imagine how it might have a life, it might have a beginning and an end.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.280
And I think that to Cedric was really, really important

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.960
and I think it's an absolutely fundamental part of what he was about.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.233
The InterAction Centre was a building that Cedric had always conceived as having a

00:09:44.233 --> 00:09:44.940
fixed life.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.505
He didn't know exactly how long it would be, but I think he probably, when it was

00:09:48.505 --> 00:09:48.760
built,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.720
wouldn't have thought it would last 30 years, let's say.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:56.280
But it was listed. In Britain, there are historic buildings laws,

00:09:56.380 --> 00:10:00.540
which mean that if a building is in danger and it is of real merit,

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.080
it can be listed after as little as 10 years.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.820
And the InterAction Centre, on a piece of land that was becoming more valuable,

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.540
this was probably the early to mid-1990s, was listed.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.920
Now, it was quite clearly a building, a structure of extraordinary importance,

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:21.760
but whether it needed to be preserved for that importance to continue is another

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:22.140
matter.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.860
And I was working in building design at the time,

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:28.738
and the editor, Paul Finch, saw this press release from English Heritage or whoever

00:10:28.738 --> 00:10:29.060
it was,

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.760
saying the new listed buildings are including the InterAction Centre.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:38.100
So Paul called Cedric up and said, Cedric, I've got very bad news.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:39.620
And Cedric said, what's that?

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.980
The InterAction Centre, it's listed.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.700
And Paul could almost feel Cedric's immediate pleasure,

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.740
but then Cedric had to regather himself,

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.800
and after, at a sharp intake of breath, he said, but that's awful!

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:01.300
So I think it was something that Cedric was very ambiguous about.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.668
he was in one sense very pleased, in another sense it went against the law of

00:11:04.668 --> 00:11:05.980
philosophy, what he stood for.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.940
Cedric was very interested in demolition.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.440
And there was a moment, again I think it must have been in the mid-1990s,

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.640
when new building regulations were introduced in Britain.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:18.080
I think they're called Construction, Design and Management, CONDAM.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.260
And part of that was that architects, when they were designing buildings,

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.900
would have to show how they would be demolished.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:28.860
This was partly a safety thing, so that someone coming in at some future point

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:33.360
would know that you couldn't do this without the building pinging or something.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.780
So Cedric was very interested in Condemn

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.580
because he thought it would make architects feel much more responsible

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.880
for the process of demolition as well as construction.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.860
I don't think there are enough traces of Cedric and his ideas

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.920
in the physical environment,

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.840
but there are lots of opportunities for making them.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.960
I think if we talk about the Magnet Project,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.260
which was one of the last major projects that Cedric made

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:00.200
with his assistant, Keiichi, in the mid-1990s.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.320
He proposed, I think it was 12 temporary fixed-life structures,

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.960
lightweight, almost, well, they were certainly prefabricated up to a point,

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:17.740
that would occupy misused or underused or even abused parts of London.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:19.380
Shaftesbury, I knew, was one.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.160
There were also some suburban locations.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.060
I think there was one over the North Circular Road in North London.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:30.260
and these were taking those bits of leftover space

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:34.200
that neither the development industry nor the planning law

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.040
nor other forms of urban management and maintenance can deal with

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:42.700
and what Cedric was doing was saying these can be first of all used,

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.540
secondly they can be used constructively

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.160
and thirdly they can be used delightfully.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.760
You can use them in such a way that you overcome problems,

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.040
that you deal with something that is otherwise an inconvenience.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:59.147
There was a story that Will also told me some years ago, which was that when Will

00:12:59.147 --> 00:12:59.840
was working

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.296
for Cedric, Cedric would say, well, the problem with buildings is that you have to

00:13:03.296 --> 00:13:03.760
walk around

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:04.040
them.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:09.060
And Will told me that story in the context of the Peckham Library in South London.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:14.310
And he said one of the reasons it has a very small footprint on the ground and then

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:14.600
a few

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:19.972
very slender, delicate columns was because he wanted to deal with Cedric's comment

00:13:19.972 --> 00:13:20.300
about

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.800
buildings being an inconvenience.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:22.680
You had to walk around them.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:27.799
He wanted to allow people to take their natural desire line route without having the

00:13:27.799 --> 00:13:30.420
inconvenience of walking around a building.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:36.298
So in some ways, I suppose, Peckham Library is one of the very few traces of the way

00:13:36.298 --> 00:13:39.820
Cedric used to think about buildings that we could see.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:46.607
But I think it's more that I would look at these parts, these sort of leftover

00:13:46.607 --> 00:13:50.120
pieces of space in urban, suburban contexts,

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:56.617
the interstices between a road junction or a bit of railway embankment or something

00:13:56.617 --> 00:13:57.400
like that,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:02.720
which is otherwise wasted space, that Cedric would always have thought about

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.620
and would probably have found some sort of constructive use for.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:11.400
So I think we can see Cedric's influence in his absence rather than in his presence.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.020
But the fact that he did make these proposals,

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:19.780
and I think Magnet is one of the most significant of that line of Cedric's thinking,

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:25.160
makes one think well actually what we do need is not necessarily Cedric to come back

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:25.880
but to think

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:31.321
about well how could we learn from what he did to make better use of these sorts of

00:14:31.321 --> 00:14:31.780
spaces.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:36.381
I think one of the reasons why we don't have enough traces of Cedric's thinking in

00:14:36.381 --> 00:14:36.800
the built

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.280
environment is because we have become so obsessed with the value of buildings, the

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:42.100
financial value

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:46.970
of buildings and also that buildings have to do something that can be defined with

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:47.920
great precision.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:52.680
Now, Cedric, I think, always would have challenged both those assumptions.

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.260
I don't think he would have denied them completely.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.620
He wasn't an anarchist.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.060
He wasn't a communist.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:01.800
So he accepted the notion of money and capital and value.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:05.290
And he also certainly accepted the fact that there was a need to do certain things

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:05.460
with

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:07.660
certain sorts of buildings.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.610
But I think he would have denied that buildings can only exist if they fulfill those

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:13.140
criteria.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:18.556
So I think the general way in which law and finance and the economy has worked has

00:15:18.556 --> 00:15:18.820
made

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:24.165
it very difficult to take some of these lessons from what Cedric was offering and to

00:15:24.165 --> 00:15:24.420
make

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:25.900
constructive use of them.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:28.520
You would almost only be able to do some of these things with some sort of

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:28.980
philanthropic

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:31.840
funding.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.380
Enabling was a very important concept for Cedric.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:39.180
I think that what he meant by enabling, again it looks like a simple word but no

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:39.720
word that

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:45.145
Cedric used in that way was ever simple. He was interested in how people can make

00:15:45.145 --> 00:15:45.480
their

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.423
lives better, not necessarily in forcing them to do things in a particular way, and

00:15:50.423 --> 00:15:50.780
that's

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:55.875
one of the reasons he was critical of the sort of puritanical streak of English

00:15:55.875 --> 00:15:56.520
socialism.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:02.038
He wanted people to have the opportunities to find out for themselves. So enabling

00:16:02.038 --> 00:16:02.240
was

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:07.892
providing, this is in very crude terms, a frame for people to collect, curate and

00:16:07.892 --> 00:16:08.380
develop

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:16.136
their own experiences and their own knowledge. It could be direct, it could not

00:16:16.136 --> 00:16:17.020
directed,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:22.804
these experiences could be as it were offered to people but there wouldn't be

00:16:22.804 --> 00:16:23.780
coercion that

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:29.101
you must do this or you must do that or you must do these things in a particular

00:16:29.101 --> 00:16:29.500
order.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:34.979
It would be an offering rather than a forcing I think. So enabling was about

00:16:34.979 --> 00:16:36.060
allowing people

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:41.100
to better themselves by the opportunities that could be created.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.840
And architecture was a small part of that,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.320
and it was a small part and a very significant part

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.780
because it could be negative.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:52.100
It could stop that process of enabling.

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:55.160
It could force you to walk on a different route

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:58.200
because the building was in the way, for example, in very simple terms.

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:01.000
Whereas a building that Cedric designed,

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:04.380
whether it was a fun palace or the InterAction Centre or magnet or whatever,

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:07.920
would actually enable a much greater series of possibilities

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.280
without forcing you to take them.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:14.480
I think it's very difficult to say what Cedric's most significant project is.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.020
If I were forced, I'd probably say The Fun Palace,

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:20.180
because I do think that's one that he worked on for a very long time.

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:24.700
He had an inspirational client collaborator in Joan Littlewood with it.

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:29.200
It was, I think, it came out of a particular moment.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:32.460
Joan, of course, was a fantastic, radical theatre director

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:36.260
who had made theatre the most significant, not her alone,

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.940
but she was part of a group of directors

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:43.440
who had made theatre the most avant-garde aspect of British culture

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.300
in the 1940s, 50s and 60s.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:49.240
And what she wanted in the Fun Palace

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:51.700
was something that was both...

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:56.100
She called it a university of the streets or some such phrase,

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:01.720
that she wanted to expand the capacity that theatre and performance had,

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:04.900
but not to limit what it would offer to theatre and performance.

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:07.700
It would move into other things as well.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.660
It could be a library, it could be a gallery.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.460
It would be something quite magnificent.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.480
And of course it was virtually buildable.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:19.380
I mean it was designed to a point where it could be built.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:23.660
There was maybe one or two technological issues that would need resolution

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.160
but there was no doubt that they could have been resolved.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:30.300
And there were also, I think in some cases, at least two sites

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.100
where it was very seriously considered.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:36.260
So it was a long-running project, a very significant project,

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.920
and one that was probably pretty close to coming off,

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:41.960
and I think it's a great tragedy that it didn't.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:45.060
Well, I think it resonates both as an idea

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.320
that I think there are a lot of people,

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.300
particularly someone like Hansel de Colbrist, the curator,

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:56.640
who looks back at the Fun Palace as perhaps not the perfect model,

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:00.440
but as an inspiration to how you might think about a gallery for the 21st century.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.500
And some years ago, in my work at the Royal Academy of Art,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:09.237
we asked a series of people to talk about what they thought was the most significant

00:19:09.237 --> 00:19:10.200
building in London.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.940
Hans Ulrich was one of them, and he picked the Fun Palace.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.880
And he gave an extraordinarily inspiring address about it.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:22.540
So I think, yes, it does resonate, not perhaps entirely within architecture,

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.900
but Cedric would never have seen it purely as an architectural device.

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:27.860
it was a social device, a cultural device.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:29.800
Another one I find absolutely intriguing,

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:33.540
and again very, very far-reaching, is the Pottery Sink Belt.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:38.300
And that, I think, came about at a particular moment,

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.300
at the point where the British higher education system was expanding.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.640
But it was expanding in a very particular way.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.060
The old universities, Oxford and Cambridge,

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:52.020
were building new colleges and the existing colleges were expanding.

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:56.380
but basically replicating the same sort of buildings and spaces

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:59.040
and certainly replicating the same sort of education

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:02.780
that they had been offering for decades, if not hundreds of years.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:06.800
The new universities of that period, like York and East Anglia,

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.280
were on suburban sites.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:14.500
Some of them were well-designed, some of them less well-designed,

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:17.200
but they were all just outside cities

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.840
that narrowly avoided getting a university in the Middle Ages.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:24.725
You could rewind the history of England and you would not be entirely surprised if

00:20:24.725 --> 00:20:29.611
York or Norwich had had a university in 1500 because they were both very important

00:20:29.611 --> 00:20:31.160
cities in the Middle Ages.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:39.934
The Thinkbelt was an attempt to get entirely away from the conventions of university

00:20:39.934 --> 00:20:44.920
work, what universities were considered to be about,

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:51.051
and to offer something that was perhaps more in tune with what was necessary at the

00:20:51.051 --> 00:20:51.420
time.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:55.160
It reused a declining bit of industrial infrastructure,

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:58.140
the old potteries, which of course is where Cedric came from,

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:01.440
where he was born, and an area he knew very well,

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:06.140
but even in the early 1960s, industry was moving out.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.860
It reused industrial infrastructure,

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:13.742
so the railway tracks would have been part of the system of transportation of goods

00:21:13.742 --> 00:21:14.360
and people,

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:19.412
it would have had the latest in information technology because Cedric was very

00:21:19.412 --> 00:21:20.060
interested

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:27.172
in that in how you could use technology to minimize the need the expense of building

00:21:27.172 --> 00:21:27.680
if you

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:32.101
could put people in touch with each other or put people in touch with ideas without

00:21:32.101 --> 00:21:32.740
a building I

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:35.538
think you would always have thought that was preferable to having to provide a

00:21:35.538 --> 00:21:36.040
building which

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:41.134
can only do it in a rather crude way and I think also the Thinkbelt was important

00:21:41.134 --> 00:21:42.140
because it would

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:47.344
have been a symbol for a form of knowledge, a form of education that was very

00:21:47.344 --> 00:21:48.020
important,

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:53.377
mainly technical education, but did not require the English class structure of

00:21:53.377 --> 00:21:54.320
public school,

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:59.700
Oxford and Cambridge, Inns of Court, House of Commons, House of Lords, which I think

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:03.100
was still the model of how English society worked.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:09.543
The Thinkbelt reached a very wide audience by being published in New Society

00:22:09.543 --> 00:22:10.140
magazine.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:16.527
His editor, Paul Barker, was an extremely wide-ranging, insightful journalist,

00:22:16.527 --> 00:22:17.100
editor.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:20.700
And among other things, he published a weekly column by Rainer Bannum.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.320
And Paul is not an architect.

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:28.277
He thinks very much about the relationship between space, spatial environment,

00:22:28.277 --> 00:22:30.860
physical environment, and social opportunity.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.696
And this, of course, was right in the territory Cedric was thinking about with the

00:22:34.696 --> 00:22:35.100
Thinkbelt.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:37.620
So a lot of work went into the Thinkbelt.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:43.410
I'm not sure whether there was a client, an assumed client, or whether there was

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:43.700
even

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:47.907
someone who was paying the bills, but Paul, through New Society, gave it a big

00:22:47.907 --> 00:22:48.500
readership.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:51.120
New Society at that time had a big circulation.

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:55.902
It was a very influential magazine of ideas, and I think anyone who wanted to be

00:22:55.902 --> 00:22:56.380
informed

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:57.380
read it.

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:01.285
And although it was sort of on the left, it was never doctrinaire on the left, and

00:23:01.285 --> 00:23:01.380
it

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:05.720
was very critical of certain aspects of left-wing as well as of right-wing thinking.

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:07.920
It was a very interesting journal of ideas.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:11.420
And I think that the Thinkbelt was absolutely up its street.

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:16.160
I remember when I was asked to talk about it some years ago,

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:19.060
I looked at the date of the magazine that's published

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:21.060
and it was about the time of my second birthday.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:23.520
So I was being asked to talk about something

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:26.740
that had been in effect designed certainly before I was conscious of it.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:29.800
I think Cedric's main agenda,

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:32.200
the things that preoccupied him most,

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:39.260
were how could you set up physical and social and informational structures

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:41.620
that allow people to fulfil themselves.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:45.620
This is very much about enabling, a word that Cedric used a lot.

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:50.920
Now, buildings architecture had possibilities for enabling

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:55.978
and it could do both by providing a physical frame within which certain things could

00:23:55.978 --> 00:23:56.400
happen,

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:59.020
in other words, a building, what we might think of as a building.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:04.140
But architecture as a way of thinking could also enable new ways of thinking.

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:06.280
Not about architecture itself.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:09.680
Architecture is very much both the metaphorical and the physical frame.

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:14.597
The fulfilment was never the building, the image of the building, the consequence of

00:24:14.597 --> 00:24:15.360
that building.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:20.283
It was actually what the building offered you that would allow you to go beyond

00:24:20.283 --> 00:24:21.620
where you were before.

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:27.820
And I think that was very much one of his central preoccupations at least.

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:31.700
And I think that is also closely related to his concept of time.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:36.300
If you're enabling people, it implies a chronology, it implies a time,

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:41.280
because enabling is a process that doesn't happen in a nanosecond.

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:43.240
It happens over a period of time.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:49.060
So the process of enabling was something that related to Cedric's interest in time.

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:55.196
And I think when he was designing buildings or thinking about what sort of buildings

00:24:55.196 --> 00:24:56.560
might be appropriate,

00:24:56.560 --> 00:25:01.846
he would think well how long can this structure continue to be an enabling

00:25:01.846 --> 00:25:02.560
structure?

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:07.886
So for example in the Potteries Thinkbelt all the structures were of relatively

00:25:07.886 --> 00:25:08.560
short life

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:13.560
because you didn't know what technology would be 15 years down the line.

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:17.421
You might have a good idea of what it might be in 5 years, possibly even in 10

00:25:17.421 --> 00:25:18.560
years, but beyond that,

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:23.528
so well within a working career, technology might change several times and in

00:25:23.528 --> 00:25:24.560
unforeseen ways.

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:31.560
So the idea of being fixed life was tied up with the need to enable.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:33.560
There are lots of anecdotes about Cedric.

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:37.560
He was a very gregarious, very ebullient character.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:38.560
There are several.

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:44.560
One is, and it was told to me by both participants, David Alford and Cedric Price.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:51.549
And David, it's fair to say, moved to the political right from being on the left as

00:25:51.549 --> 00:25:52.560
a young man.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:56.646
young man. And Cedric of course stayed on the left although he was always quite

00:25:56.646 --> 00:25:57.060
critical

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:02.397
of certain left wing positions or conventionally left wing positions. And David was

00:26:02.397 --> 00:26:03.040
also quite

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:07.701
a combative character. He liked picking an argument. And David and Cedric were

00:26:07.701 --> 00:26:08.060
having

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:12.405
a drink somewhere which was not an uncommon occurrence and they started talking

00:26:12.405 --> 00:26:12.680
about

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:18.702
politics and David was very provocative particularly with people he thought were

00:26:18.702 --> 00:26:19.680
left wing and

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.560
and he enjoyed the argument.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:27.260
And he, probably having bought a rather fine bottle of wine,

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:28.780
which he would also do,

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:33.580
he said, Cedric, well, how do you think, in a socialist society,

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:35.300
we would divide up wine?

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:37.480
And Cedric just said, ration it,

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:39.160
at which point they came to blows.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:41.780
I think Cedric was very generous,

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:43.880
and he was generous in a particular way.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:46.540
He was generous with his time and he was generous with his intellect.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:49.940
and he was also, if you like, too generous with his cigars and brandy.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:56.740
And that meant a huge amount to me when I was in my 20s, 30s,

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:58.820
and that Cedric would be there,

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:02.560
not just because he was a very significant and very well-known figure,

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:05.680
but because he would actually engage with what I was interested in.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:11.860
And there was one time when Cedric had offered me use of his library,

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.560
or the run of his library with some books

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:16.320
that he thought would interest me in my work on F.R.S. York.

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:21.160
was very useful and I was upstairs in the white room and we would Cedric

00:27:21.160 --> 00:27:25.220
started work early in the morning when he wasn't having breakfast 730 830 so I

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:29.580
got there early in the morning and Cedric was there cooking breakfast if I

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:33.280
remember rightly always in the office and the white room had a little baby

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:37.600
bedding stove and and he would if you were very lucky he would cook you

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:42.380
breakfast if you had breakfast you generally had to have brandy in a cigar

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:47.380
as well. And there were also the books. So I started and I looked at these books and

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:51.890
thought, oh, this is going to be quite a lot of work to go through this and I want

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:52.220
to get

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:57.736
on with it. And Cedric said, well, would you like a glass of brandy? This was 8.30

00:27:57.736 --> 00:27:58.140
in the

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:01.785
morning. I said, well, actually, Cedric, not today, if you don't mind. He said,

00:28:01.785 --> 00:28:02.200
well, I'm

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:07.619
going to have one. And I said, yes, please do. And then he went downstairs to his

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:08.020
office

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:11.920
and 20 minutes later he came back and said, are you sure you won't have a brandy?

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:16.160
And I said, no, actually, Cedric, I'd really rather not just at the moment, maybe

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:16.440
later.

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.160
So he went down, 20 minutes later, he's up again, what about a cigar?

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.620
And I said, no, Cedric, really, I'm actually trying to work.

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:23.960
And then he went down again.

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:27.880
And then 20 minutes after that, he was back again, glass of brandy, cigar.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.866
He said, well, if you're not going to have a brandy or a cigar, I'm going to have

00:28:30.866 --> 00:28:31.040
both.

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:34.600
And I realised that it was going to be very difficult to do this work

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:36.540
because Cedric actually wanted to chat.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:42.000
So there was a great generosity and a great sense of fun with him.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:45.360
I mean, there were times when we talked about very serious things,

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:46.780
and of course there were times when we talked about things

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.540
which were not inherently fun,

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:51.420
but there was always a sense with Cedric of optimism,

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:53.020
and I think that was really important,

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.780
and that came through his personality,

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:57.320
I think it came through his relationships with other people,

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:59.460
you know, his friends, people who worked with him,

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:01.160
that there was always a sense of fun,

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:06.940
as well as a sense of a serious purpose underneath the fun,

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:08.100
or connected to the fun.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:13.054
The white room was a space at the top of Cedric's office in Alpha Place, which was

00:29:13.054 --> 00:29:13.900
painted white.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.300
Floor, ceiling, walls.

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:18.800
It was relatively sparsely furnished.

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:22.229
There was a table, there were a few chairs, there was a little stove and I think a

00:29:22.229 --> 00:29:22.960
fridge and a sink.

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:28.053
It must have been at some stage fitted out as a small flat for the rest of the

00:29:28.053 --> 00:29:28.540
offices.

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:32.186
I'm not sure that Cedric ever used it like that, but he certainly used it to do some

00:29:32.186 --> 00:29:32.660
entertaining.

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:34.660
And it was a sort of sacrosanct space.

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:35.940
It was where work was not done.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:37.560
The other floors were where people worked.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:41.660
The White Room was where you went for meetings, conversation,

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:44.880
where your brain would certainly be picked.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:49.360
It was not project-based work, I suppose.

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:53.820
It was intellectual work of a more abstract nature.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:56.300
I don't think the White Room was a sacred place.

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:58.460
I don't think Cedric thought in those terms.

00:29:58.840 --> 00:30:00.160
It was certainly a sanctuary.

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:02.080
It was a place where he would go.

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:04.560
I think he spent a certain amount of time there on his own,

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:06.620
reading, reading newspaper.

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:12.528
He never had a telephone there, that was the other thing, that it was free from

00:30:12.528 --> 00:30:13.500
interference.

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:18.120
It was, yes, certainly to be invited there was quite an honour and a pleasure.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:21.777
I'm sure there are people who would like to have been invited who weren't, but I

00:30:21.777 --> 00:30:21.960
also

00:30:21.960 --> 00:30:28.272
think that Cedric, if someone wrote to him or telephoned him and gave him enough to

00:30:28.272 --> 00:30:28.880
interest

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:33.000
him in them, they would be invited to have a conversation.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.340
That would probably be in the White Room.

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:38.998
For Cedric the relationship between people as individuals and institutions was

00:30:38.998 --> 00:30:39.420
extremely

00:30:39.420 --> 00:30:40.500
important.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.220
He wasn't an anarchist.

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.520
He thought that there needed to be institutions.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.860
He thought that there was knowledge that should be made available.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:52.140
It shouldn't be done on an exclusive basis or an elitist basis.

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:57.640
But you weren't, I think in Cedric's view, born with everything you needed to know.

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:02.440
You didn't know, you hadn't met everyone you might need to meet or would benefit

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:03.220
from meeting.

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:08.100
So there was a role for certain sorts of institutions in society.

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:12.559
I think what made Cedric a really important thinker, and it runs through so much of

00:31:12.559 --> 00:31:12.720
his

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:17.280
work, was the idea that institutions don't have to perpetuate themselves.

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.500
You create institutions for a particular purpose.

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:24.398
It might be educational, it might be governments, it might be informational, it

00:31:24.398 --> 00:31:25.080
might be to

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:27.660
do with leisure and fun.

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:34.720
But they should never be open-ended in their time.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:39.230
So a project like Pop-up Parliament was to try and move the idea of Parliament, or

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:39.560
indeed

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:46.893
move the idea of a democratic, organisational governing body away from some of the

00:31:46.893 --> 00:31:47.340
older

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:50.530
traditions associated with it into something that was more responsive and more

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:50.980
appropriate

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.300
for the period it was done.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:56.753
Now I personally think there's quite a lot to be said for certain sorts of

00:31:56.753 --> 00:31:57.220
traditions

00:31:57.220 --> 00:32:03.252
to remain, but I'm also very receptive to Cedric's ideas that certain sorts of

00:32:03.252 --> 00:32:04.180
institutions

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:06.980
in particular should be limited in time.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:12.420
I remember having a conversation with him about something that happened when it was

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:18.281
realised that there were far fewer house fires than there had been, and therefore

00:32:18.281 --> 00:32:18.860
the fire

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:24.620
Brigade's role was changing because it wasn't called out to house fires as much.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:30.380
And the Fire Brigade had made a play to take over the Ambulance Service, where

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:34.940
the Ambulance Service was increasing in its work and the Fire Brigade was

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:39.560
declining. In order to perpetuate the existence of an institution called the

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:43.100
Fire Service, it tried to take over the Ambulance Service, when in Cedric's view,

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:45.740
if they were going to murder a toilet, it should have been the other way around.

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:49.180
The ambulance service was more innovative in how it was using ambulances.

00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:53.600
It had a need that was increasing or changing,

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.000
whereas the need for the fire brigade was declining,

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.820
in its conventional sense at least.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:03.860
And I think that's part of what he was talking about with institutions,

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:08.960
that you don't need to feel they have to be perpetuated beyond their natural life.

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:13.395
Now, that's one side, I think, of what I was trying to explain in the Guardian

00:33:13.395 --> 00:33:13.840
obituary.

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:19.425
The other side, I think, is very much that the test of an institution, of the value

00:33:19.425 --> 00:33:25.010
of an institution, was the degree to which it would enable someone to improve their

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:29.720
own life, their own status, their own experience, their own enjoyment.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:35.318
And if an institution didn't offer that, then I think he would have thought it had

00:33:35.318 --> 00:33:35.980
no purpose.

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:41.648
That didn't mean that every institution had to offer equal amounts of joy, pleasure,

00:33:41.648 --> 00:33:43.740
delight, democracy to everyone,

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:49.223
but they had to have a constituency of people who they were enabling to do something

00:33:49.223 --> 00:33:49.660
better.

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:54.688
I think there's an important insight into how Cedric thought about institutions and

00:33:54.688 --> 00:33:55.640
their social role

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:58.800
in something he wrote, I think, in the early 1980s,

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:03.630
which was over hot imagery in built form appears to be in inverse proportion to the

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:05.900
importance of the activities it houses.

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:12.443
And I think that sums up something about what Cedric thought about institutions that

00:34:12.443 --> 00:34:18.522
choose to house themselves in a purely symbolic home and not thinking about the

00:34:18.522 --> 00:34:22.600
degree of time or the capacity for enabling they had.

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:29.563
when I was thinking about the Thinkbelt for the supercrit I looked at the

00:34:29.563 --> 00:34:31.280
publication of the

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:35.194
architectural review used to do a sort of review of buildings of the year and I did

00:34:35.194 --> 00:34:35.760
two or three

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:39.692
years either side of the think Thinkbelt on that to see what what contemporary

00:34:39.692 --> 00:34:40.700
British architecture

00:34:40.700 --> 00:34:45.226
was in effect and one of them was a very beautiful building for Newhall College in

00:34:45.226 --> 00:34:46.220
Cambridge designed

00:34:46.220 --> 00:34:49.649
by Chamberlain Powell and Bong who were good architects they designed the Barbican

00:34:49.649 --> 00:34:49.900
Centre

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:55.620
and Golden Lane, but it is absolutely what Cedric would scathingly have called Roman

00:34:55.620 --> 00:35:01.619
architecture with 13 amp sockets. And I think by that he meant an architecture that

00:35:01.619 --> 00:35:01.980
could

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:06.564
have been conceived 2,000 years earlier, and it was only redeemed from modern use

00:35:06.564 --> 00:35:06.960
because

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:11.380
somehow the technology had been inserted into it. Well, Pop-Up Parliament, I think,

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:11.540
was

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:15.186
a project that Cedric did in the middle of the 1960s. Again, I think it was in

00:35:15.186 --> 00:35:15.700
association

00:35:15.700 --> 00:35:18.360
with New Society magazine and Paul Barker, the editor.

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:23.480
And what it was, was a way of thinking about how a parliament could act

00:35:23.480 --> 00:35:30.000
outside or not contained by the very, very heavy symbolism

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.660
of the British Houses of Parliament,

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:35.700
which was a huge building, not that old,

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:36.900
well, the oldest parts were very old,

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:38.640
but most of what you see in the Houses of Parliament

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.940
is mid to even late 19th century.

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:44.400
And in living memory, certainly in the 1960s, it had been bombed.

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:46.920
the House of Commons, the Chamber of the House of Commons was destroyed.

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:50.180
And Winston Churchill, as Prime Minister, said,

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:52.540
we must rebuild it exactly as it was,

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:57.060
because that is how we will continue our democratic traditions.

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:01.300
And I think what Paul Barker and Cedric were trying to get away from

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:05.040
was that sort of replicating of tradition

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.100
and that sort of continuation of tradition,

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:10.420
as opposed to what they might have called essence,

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:13.040
which was democracy and democratic action.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:17.852
Now could a different sort of building, a different sort of spatial system, spatial

00:36:17.852 --> 00:36:21.620
relations and of information flows offer that in a different way?

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:23.300
And I think that's what pop-up parliament was about.

00:36:23.700 --> 00:36:28.720
It didn't need to be a huge stone-built building that wouldn't move.

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:33.259
And in some ways, and I'm not sure that they would have accepted this, but it was

00:36:33.259 --> 00:36:37.080
going back to the roots of parliament, which was when it was peripatetic.

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:41.295
Parliament used to travel around the country and would meet wherever the king called

00:36:41.295 --> 00:36:41.440
it.

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:45.800
And it was only in the 14th, 15th century that Parliament became fixed and

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:47.980
associated with one particular place.

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:51.640
And it was only in the 19th century that it got a purpose-designed building.

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:56.040
So in fact, what they were doing was actually looking back, perhaps, in some ways,

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:59.360
or what they were doing had some analogies with the history of Parliament itself.

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:03.011
I'm not sure that for Cedric the historical resonances would have been the most

00:37:03.011 --> 00:37:03.600
important thing.

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:08.058
I think for Cedric the really important thing would have been to use the most

00:37:08.058 --> 00:37:09.280
appropriate technology

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:11.740
in building materials and information technology

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:15.860
to allow democracy to act better,

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:19.880
particularly by allowing elected, mandated members of parliament

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:24.200
to do their work in a more open and a more efficient way.

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:29.380
Well, Cedric certainly challenged traditional architectural aesthetics.

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:33.360
He was incredibly knowledgeable about architecture of the past,

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:35.340
and this is something that a lot of people don't understand.

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:39.480
you could mention a relatively obscure 18th century country house.

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:41.820
Cedric would know it, he would have been there,

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:43.280
he would be able to draw it,

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:45.520
he would tell you a lot about its history.

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:49.220
So he had a phenomenal knowledge of the traditions of architecture,

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:51.380
but he was never uncritical of them,

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.560
and he certainly rejected any sense

00:37:53.560 --> 00:37:56.740
that what had worked in the 18th century or even the 19th century

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:58.040
or even the early 20th century

00:37:58.040 --> 00:38:01.120
would have worked for the later 20th century or into the 21st century.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:05.160
So he was always challenging the traditions and the conventions

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.420
but from a position of knowing what they were,

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:08.860
and knowing what they had achieved,

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:10.840
and knowing what their strengths were as well as their weaknesses.

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:16.080
So was Cedric rejecting conventional architectural aesthetics?

00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:17.740
Yes, I think he was.

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:20.740
I think Cedric's comment about over-hot imagery

00:38:20.740 --> 00:38:25.320
being in inverse proportion to the importance of the activity it houses

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:30.780
is a summing up of his rejection or his attack

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.740
on the conventions of architectural aesthetics.

00:38:32.740 --> 00:38:38.255
For Cedric, aesthetics came from a way of thinking and a way of doing and a way of

00:38:38.255 --> 00:38:38.860
enabling.

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:41.620
I think he was a phenomenal artist.

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:44.920
I think his visual sense was extraordinary.

00:38:45.580 --> 00:38:49.900
I think you only need to look at the way he built up his imagery,

00:38:50.440 --> 00:38:55.080
using drawings, using photocopies, using rubber stamps.

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:59.900
Everything was extraordinarily precise and beautifully worked out.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:04.349
And I think that's one reason why a lot of contemporary artists are so interested in

00:39:04.349 --> 00:39:04.720
Cedric,

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:08.900
because he was a profoundly visual and aesthetic thinker,

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:14.819
but he did so largely outside or beyond the conventions of the discipline of

00:39:14.819 --> 00:39:15.780
architecture.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:20.120
I think the relevance of Cedric now comes in several different parts.

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:25.280
I think the body of work that he produced is still extraordinarily important,

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.500
and even if some of it's now 50 years old,

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:31.400
there are still important and interesting things we can find and we can look at.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:34.620
And that is a challenge to how we think about architecture.

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:37.240
And I think that will always be there.

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:42.160
Cedric's, I think, perhaps his most lasting contribution,

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:44.820
certainly one of the most significant contributions he made,

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:49.280
is to show that you could always challenge convention.

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:51.260
You never had to accept it.

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:54.860
So I think that's something that architects could actually do an awful lot more of

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:56.740
and indeed sacred clients very often.

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:02.460
I think, so the body of work still has extraordinary relevance.

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:06.040
I think there are people who worked with Cedric and knew Cedric well

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:08.120
who are producing very important work.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:09.720
Coolhouse is certainly one of them.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:14.660
And I don't think you could reduce his practice to Cedric's or Cedric's to his,

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:19.721
but I think his ability to bring together pieces of information from outside

00:40:19.721 --> 00:40:20.520
architecture

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:26.780
and to develop programmes as much as buildings owes quite a lot to Cedric

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:32.279
and I think in some ways that's a continuation of ways of thinking that Cedric set

00:40:32.279 --> 00:40:32.480
up.
